+### 2.8.1 Short-Circuit Logical Operators
+
+```nanz
+fun in_range(c: u8) -> bool {
+    return (c >= 65 && c <= 90) || (c >= 97 && c <= 122)
+}
+```
+
+`&&` and `||` evaluate the right operand only when the left one demands it —
+`a && b` skips `b` when `a` is false, `a || b` skips `b` when `a` is true.
+Side effects in the right operand are therefore not guaranteed to run:
+
+```nanz
+if flag && note(true) { ... }   // note() runs only when flag is true
+```
+
+Both operators bind looser than the bitwise operators and the comparisons,
+so the parentheses in the example above are optional; they are kept for
+readability. The result is a canonical `bool` (`1`/`0`), so it can be used as
+a value, as an `if`/`while` condition, or as an operand of another logical
+operator. Operands may be of any scalar type — non-bool operands are tested
+against zero, as in C.
+
